24, 14. Cf. Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 747; Geldner, Vedische Studien, 1, 280, n. 1.\nAs sa-patna, ‘rival,’ is unmistakably formed from sa-patnī, ‘co-wife.’", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-nagnee", "mahaa-nagni", "mahaa-nagnii", "mahaanagnee", "mahaanagni", "mahaanagnii", "mahā", "mahā-nagni", "mahā-nagnī", "mahānagni", "mahānagnī", "nagnee", "nagni", "nagnii", "nagnī", "नग्नि", "नग्नी", "महा", "महा-नग्नि", "महानग्नि", "महानग्नी", "నగ్ని", "నగ్నీ", "మహా", "మహా-నగ్ని", "మహా-నగ్నీ", "మహానగ్ని", "మహానగ్నీ", "ನಗ್ನಿ", "ನಗ್ನೀ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ನಗ್ನಿ", "ಮಹಾ-ನಗ್ನೀ", "ಮಹಾನಗ್ನಿ", "ಮಹಾನಗ್ನೀ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-nagnee", "mahaa-nagni", "mahaa-nagnii", "mahaanagnee", "mahaanagni", "mahaanagnii", "mahā", "mahā-nagni", "mahā-nagnī", "mahānagni", "mahānagnī", "nagnee", "nagni", "nagnii", "nagnī", "nagni", "nagnI", "mahA", "mahA-nagni", "mahAnagni", "mahAnagnI", "నగ్ని", "నగ్నీ", "మహా", "మహా-నగ్ని", "మహా-నగ్నీ", "మహానగ్ని", "మహానగ్నీ", "ನಗ್ನಿ", "ನಗ್ನೀ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ನಗ್ನಿ", "ಮಹಾ-ನಗ್ನೀ", "ಮಹಾನಗ್ನಿ", "ಮಹಾನಗ್ನೀ"]}], "maha-naga": [{"id": "mahA-nAga", "headword": "महा-नाग", "headword_slp1": "mahA-nAga", "fold": "maha-naga", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-nāga, a ‘great snake,’ is mentioned in the Śatapatha brāhmaṇa (xi. 2, 7, 12), where it is plainly mythical.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-naaga", "mahaanaaga", "mahā", "mahā-nāga", "mahānāga", "naaga", "nāga", "नाग", "महा", "महानाग", "నాగ", "మహా", "మహా-నాగ", "మహానాగ", "ನಾಗ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ನಾಗ", "ಮಹಾನಾಗ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-naaga", "mahaanaaga", "mahā", "mahā-nāga", "mahānāga", "naaga", "nāga", "nAga", "mahA", "mahAnAga", "నాగ", "మహా", "మహా-నాగ", "మహానాగ", "ನಾಗ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ನಾಗ", "ಮಹಾನಾಗ"]}], "maha-niraswa": [{"id": "mahA-nirazwa", "headword": "महा-निरष्ट", "headword_slp1": "mahA-nirazwa", "fold": "maha-niraswa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-niraṣṭa, a ‘great castrated’ ox, is mentioned as the dakṣiṇā, or ‘sacrificial fee,’ in the house of the sūta at the rājasūya (‘royal consecration’) in the yajurveda Saṃhitās. Cf. anaḍvāh and Go.\nFoot notes taittirīya Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 1; kāṭhaka Saṃhitā, xv. 4, 9; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 5.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-niraShTa", "mahaaniraShTa", "mahā", "mahā-niraṣṭa", "mahāniraṣṭa", "niraShTa", "niraṣṭa", "निरष्ट", "महा", "महानिरष्ट", "నిరష్ట", "మహా", "మహా-నిరష్ట", "మహానిరష్ట", "ನಿರಷ್ಟ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ನಿರಷ್ಟ", "ಮಹಾನಿರಷ್ಟ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-niraShTa", "mahaaniraShTa", "mahā", "mahā-niraṣṭa", "mahāniraṣṭa", "niraShTa", "niraṣṭa", "nirazwa", "mahA", "mahAnirazwa", "నిరష్ట", "మహా", "మహా-నిరష్ట", "మహానిరష్ట", "ನಿರಷ್ಟ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ನಿರಷ್ಟ", "ಮಹಾನಿರಷ್ಟ"]}], "maha-paTa": [{"id": "mahA-paTa", "headword": "महा-पथ", "headword_slp1": "mahA-paTa", "fold": "maha-paTa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-patha in the Brāhmaṇas denotes the ‘high road’ between two villages.\nFoot notes aitareya brāhmaṇa, iv. 17, 8; Chāndogya upaniṣad, viii. 6, 2. Cf. Weber, Indische Studien, 1, 271, n.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-patha", "mahaapatha", "mahā", "mahā-patha", "mahāpatha", "patha", "पथ", "महा", "महापथ", "పథ", "మహా", "మహా-పథ", "మహాపథ", "ಪಥ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಪಥ", "ಮಹಾಪಥ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-patha", "mahaapatha", "mahā", "mahā-patha", "mahāpatha", "patha", "paTa", "mahA", "mahApaTa", "పథ", "మహా", "మహా-పథ", "మహాపథ", "ಪಥ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಪಥ", "ಮಹಾಪಥ"]}], "maha-pura": [{"id": "mahA-pura", "headword": "महा-पुर", "headword_slp1": "mahA-pura", "fold": "maha-pura", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-pura in the yajurveda Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas denotes a great fortress. Probably the only difference between the pur and the mahāpura was size.\nFoot notes taittirīya Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 3, 1; kāṭhaka Saṃhitā, xxiv. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 1.\naitareya brāhmaṇa, i. 23, 2; Gopatha brāhmaṇa, ii. 2, 7.\ntaittirīya Saṃhitā, vi. 2, 3, 1; kāṭhaka Saṃhitā, xxiv. 10; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 1.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-pura", "mahaapura", "mahā", "mahā-pura", "mahāpura", "pura", "पुर", "महा", "महापुर", "పుర", "మహా", "మహా-పుర", "మహాపుర", "ಪುರ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಪುರ", "ಮಹಾಪುರ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-pura", "mahaapura", "mahā", "mahā-pura", "mahāpura", "pura", "pura", "mahA", "mahApura", "పుర", "మహా", "మహా-పుర", "మహాపుర", "ಪುರ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಪುರ", "ಮಹಾಪುರ"]}], "maha-brahmaRa": [{"id": "mahA-brAhmaRa", "headword": "महा-ब्राह्मण", "headword_slp1": "mahA-brAhmaRa", "fold": "maha-brahmaRa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-brāhmaṇa, a ‘great Brahmin,’ is found in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka upaniṣad (ii. 1, 19. 22) denoting a Brahmin of great consequence. Cf. maharṣi.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["braahmaNa", "brāhmaṇa", "mahaa", "mahaa-braahmaNa", "mahaabraahmaNa", "mahā", "mahā-brāhmaṇa", "mahābrāhmaṇa", "ब्राह्मण", "महा", "महाब्राह्मण", "బ్రాహ్మణ", "మహా", "మహా-బ్రాహ్మణ", "మహాబ్రాహ్మణ", "ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ", "ಮಹಾಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["braahmaNa", "brāhmaṇa", "mahaa", "mahaa-braahmaNa", "mahaabraahmaNa", "mahā", "mahā-brāhmaṇa", "mahābrāhmaṇa", "brAhmaRa", "mahA", "mahAbrAhmaRa", "బ్రాహ్మణ", "మహా", "మహా-బ్రాహ్మణ", "మహాబ్రాహ్మణ", "ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ", "ಮಹಾಬ್ರಾಹ್ಮಣ"]}], "mahaBiseka": [{"id": "mahABizeka", "headword": "महाभिषेक", "headword_slp1": "mahABizeka", "fold": "mahaBiseka", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahābhiṣeka, ‘great consecration,’ is mentioned in the aitareya brāhmaṇa, and described as a ceremony performed for great kings, a list of whom is given. It is equivalent to the rājasūya.\nFoot notes viii. 14, 4; 19, 2. Cf. Weber, Episches im vedischen Ritual, 8. The list is janamejaya pārikṣita, whose friend was tura kāvaṣeya; śāryāta mānava and cyavana bhārgava; Satānīka sātrājita and Somaśuṣma bhārgava; ambarīṣa and parvata and nārada; Yudhāṃśrauṣṭi augrasainya and the same two ṛṣis; viśvakarman bhauvana and kaśyapa; sudās paijavana and vasiṣṭha; marutta āvikṣita and saṃvarta; aṅga vairocana and udamaya ātreya; bharata dauḥṣanti and dīrghatamas māmateya; durmukha pāñcāla and bṛhaduktha; Atyarāti jānaṃtapi and vāsiṣṭha sātyahavya.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaabhiSheka", "mahābhiṣeka", "మహాభిషేక", "ಮಹಾಭಿಷೇಕ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaabhiSheka", "mahābhiṣeka", "మహాభిషేక", "ಮಹಾಭಿಷೇಕ"]}], "maha-Buta": [{"id": "mahA-BUta", "headword": "महा-भूत", "headword_slp1": "mahA-BUta", "fold": "maha-Buta", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-bhūta in the nirukta (xiv. 5, 10) and the aitareya upaniṣad (iii. 2, 3) denotes the ‘gross elements’ (earth, water, fire, air, ether).", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["bhoota", "bhuuta", "bhūta", "mahaa", "mahaa-bhoota", "mahaa-bhuuta", "mahaabhoota", "mahaabhuuta", "mahā", "mahā-bhūta", "mahābhūta", "भूत", "महा", "महाभूत", "భూత", "మహా", "మహా-భూత", "మహాభూత", "ಭೂತ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಭೂತ", "ಮಹಾಭೂತ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["bhoota", "bhuuta", "bhūta", "mahaa", "mahaa-bhoota", "mahaa-bhuuta", "mahaabhoota", "mahaabhuuta", "mahā", "mahā-bhūta", "mahābhūta", "BUta", "mahA", "mahABUta", "భూత", "మహా", "మహా-భూత", "మహాభూత", "ಭೂತ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಭೂತ", "ಮಹಾಭೂತ"]}], "maha-matsya": [{"id": "mahA-matsya", "headword": "महा-मत्स्य", "headword_slp1": "mahA-matsya", "fold": "maha-matsya", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-matsya, a ‘great fish,’ is mentioned in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka upaniṣad (iv. 3, 18).", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-matsya", "mahaamatsya", "mahā", "mahā-matsya", "mahāmatsya", "matsya", "मत्स्य", "महा", "महामत्स्य", "మత్స్య", "మహా", "మహా-మత్స్య", "మహామత్స్య", "ಮತ್ಸ್ಯ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಮತ್ಸ್ಯ", "ಮಹಾಮತ್ಸ್ಯ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-matsya", "mahaamatsya", "mahā", "mahā-matsya", "mahāmatsya", "matsya", "matsya", "mahA", "mahAmatsya", "మత్స్య", "మహా", "మహా-మత్స్య", "మహామత్స్య", "ಮತ್ಸ್ಯ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಮತ್ಸ್ಯ", "ಮಹಾಮತ್ಸ್ಯ"]}], "maha-meru": [{"id": "mahA-meru", "headword": "महा-मेरु", "headword_slp1": "mahA-meru", "fold": "maha-meru", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-meru, ‘great meru,’ is the name of a mountain in the taittirīya Āraṇyaka.\nFoot notes i. 7, 1, 3. Cf. Weber, Indische Studien, 1, 78; 3, 123.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-meru", "mahaameru", "mahā", "mahā-meru", "mahāmeru", "meru", "महा", "महामेरु", "मेरु", "మహా", "మహా-మేరు", "మహామేరు", "మేరు", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಮೇರು", "ಮಹಾಮೇರು", "ಮೇರು"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-meru", "mahaameru", "mahā", "mahā-meru", "mahāmeru", "meru", "mahA", "mahAmeru", "meru", "మహా", "మహా-మేరు", "మహామేరు", "మేరు", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಮೇರು", "ಮಹಾಮೇರು", "ಮೇರು"]}], "maha-raTa": [{"id": "mahA-raTa", "headword": "महा-रथ", "headword_slp1": "mahA-raTa", "fold": "maha-raTa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-ratha, ‘having a great car’--i.e., ‘a great chariot fighter,’ is an epithet of the hero who is prayed for in the ritual of the aśvamedha (‘horse sacrifice’) in the yajurveda Saṃhitās.\nFoot notes taittirīya Saṃhitā, vii. 5, 18, 1; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxii. 22.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-ratha", "mahaaratha", "mahā", "mahā-ratha", "mahāratha", "ratha", "महा", "महारथ", "रथ", "మహా", "మహా-రథ", "మహారథ", "రథ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ರಥ", "ಮಹಾರಥ", "ರಥ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-ratha", "mahaaratha", "mahā", "mahā-ratha", "mahāratha", "ratha", "mahA", "mahAraTa", "raTa", "మహా", "మహా-రథ", "మహారథ", "రథ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ರಥ", "ಮಹಾರಥ", "ರಥ"]}], "maha-raja": [{"id": "mahA-rAja", "headword": "महा-राज", "headword_slp1": "mahA-rAja", "fold": "maha-raja", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-rāja, a ‘great king,’ is frequently referred to in the Brāhmaṇas. It seems to mean no more than a king, or rather perhaps a reigning and powerful king, as opposed to a mere prince, who would also be called rājan.\nFoot notes aitareya brāhmaṇa, vii. 34, 9; kauṣītaki brāhmaṇa, v. 5; Śatapatha brāhmaṇa, i. 6, 4, 21; ii. 5, 4, 9; Bṛhadāraṇyaka upaniṣad, ii. 1, 19 et seq.; Maitrāyaṇī upaniṣad, ii. 1, etc.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-raaja", "mahaaraaja", "mahā", "mahā-rāja", "mahārāja", "raaja", "rāja", "महा", "महाराज", "राज", "మహా", "మహా-రాజ", "మహారాజ", "రాజ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ರಾಜ", "ಮಹಾರಾಜ", "ರಾಜ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-raaja", "mahaaraaja", "mahā", "mahā-rāja", "mahārāja", "raaja", "rāja", "mahA", "mahArAja", "rAja", "మహా", "మహా-రాజ", "మహారాజ", "రాజ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ರಾಜ", "ಮಹಾರಾಜ", "ರಾಜ"]}], "maha-ratra": [{"id": "mahA-rAtra", "headword": "महा-रात्र", "headword_slp1": "mahA-rAtra", "fold": "maha-ratra", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-rātra, ‘advanced night,’ is a phrase found in the kauṣītaki brāhmaṇa and the Sūtras to denote the latter part of the night, after midnight and before dawn.\nFoot notes ii. 9; xi. 8.\nśāṅkhāyana śrauta sūtra, vi. 2, 1; xvii. 7, 1, etc.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-raatra", "mahaaraatra", "mahā", "mahā-rātra", "mahārātra", "raatra", "rātra", "महा", "महारात्र", "रात्र", "మహా", "మహా-రాత్ర", "మహారాత్ర", "రాత్ర", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ರಾತ್ರ", "ಮಹಾರಾತ್ರ", "ರಾತ್ರ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-raatra", "mahaaraatra", "mahā", "mahā-rātra", "mahārātra", "raatra", "rātra", "mahA", "mahArAtra", "rAtra", "మహా", "మహా-రాత్ర", "మహారాత్ర", "రాత్ర", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ರಾತ್ರ", "ಮಹಾರಾತ್ರ", "ರಾತ್ರ"]}], "maharRava": [{"id": "mahArRava", "headword": "महार्णव", "headword_slp1": "mahArRava", "fold": "maharRava", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahārṇava, a ‘great ocean,’ is a phrase not found before the late Maitrāyaṇī upaniṣad (i. 4), where the drying up of ‘great oceans’ is one of the marvels enumerated. Cf. samudra.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaarNava", "mahārṇava", "మహార్ణవ", "ಮಹಾರ್ಣವ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaarNava", "mahārṇava", "మహార్ణవ", "ಮಹಾರ್ಣವ"]}], "maha-vira": [{"id": "mahA-vIra", "headword": "महा-वीर", "headword_slp1": "mahA-vIra", "fold": "maha-vira", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-vīra (‘great hero’) is the name in the later Saṃhitās and the Brāhmaṇas of a large earthenware pot which could be placed on the fire, and which was especially employed at the introductory soma ceremony called Pravargya.\nFoot notes Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xix. 14; Satapatha brāhmaṇa, xiv. 1, 2, 9, 17; 3, 1, 13; 4, 16; 2, 2, 13. 40; pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa, ix. 10, 1; kauṣītaki brāhmaṇa, viii. 3, 7, etc.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-veera", "mahaa-viira", "mahaaveera", "mahaaviira", "mahā", "mahā-vīra", "mahāvīra", "veera", "viira", "vīra", "महा", "महावीर", "वीर", "మహా", "మహా-వీర", "మహావీర", "వీర", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ವೀರ", "ಮಹಾವೀರ", "ವೀರ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-veera", "mahaa-viira", "mahaaveera", "mahaaviira", "mahā", "mahā-vīra", "mahāvīra", "veera", "viira", "vīra", "mahA", "mahAvIra", "vIra", "మహా", "మహా-వీర", "మహావీర", "వీర", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ವೀರ", "ಮಹಾವೀರ", "ವೀರ"]}], "maha-vfksa": [{"id": "mahA-vfkza", "headword": "महा-वृक्ष", "headword_slp1": "mahA-vfkza", "fold": "maha-vfksa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-vṛkṣa a ‘great tree,’ is mentioned sometimes in the pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa (vii. 6, 15; xiv. 1, 12) and in the Sūtras.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-vRRikSha", "mahaa-vRikSha", "mahaa-vRkSha", "mahaa-vRukSha", "mahaa-vRxa", "mahaavRRikSha", "mahaavRikSha", "mahaavRkSha", "mahaavRukSha", "mahaavRxa", "mahā", "mahā-vṛkṣa", "mahāvṛkṣa", "vRRikSha", "vRikSha", "vRkSha", "vRukSha", "vRxa", "vṛkṣa", "महा", "महावृक्ष", "वृक्ष", "మహా", "మహా-వృక్ష", "మహావృక్ష", "వృక్ష", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ವೃಕ್ಷ", "ಮಹಾವೃಕ್ಷ", "ವೃಕ್ಷ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-vRRikSha", "mahaa-vRikSha", "mahaa-vRkSha", "mahaa-vRukSha", "mahaa-vRxa", "mahaavRRikSha", "mahaavRikSha", "mahaavRkSha", "mahaavRukSha", "mahaavRxa", "mahā", "mahā-vṛkṣa", "mahāvṛkṣa", "vRRikSha", "vRikSha", "vRkSha", "vRukSha", "vRxa", "vṛkṣa", "mahA", "mahAvfkza", "vfkza", "మహా", "మహా-వృక్ష", "మహావృక్ష", "వృక్ష", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ವೃಕ್ಷ", "ಮಹಾವೃಕ್ಷ", "ವೃಕ್ಷ"]}], "maha-vfsa": [{"id": "mahA-vfza", "headword": "महा-वृष", "headword_slp1": "mahA-vfza", "fold": "maha-vfsa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-vṛṣa is the name of a tribe mentioned along with the Mūjavants in the Atharvaveda as a locality to which fever is to be relegated. It is reasonable to suppose that they were northerners, though Bloomfield suggests that the name may be chosen more for its sound and sense (as ‘of mighty strength’ to resist the disease) than for its geographical position. In the Chāndogya upaniṣad the place raikvaparṇa is said to be in the mahāvṛṣa country. The king of the Mahāvṛṣas in the Jaiminīya upaniṣad brāhmaṇa is said to be Hṛtsvāśaya. The Mahāvṛṣas are also known from a mantra in the baudhāyana śrauta sūtra.\nFoot notes v. 22, 4, 5, 8.\nHymns of the Atharvaveda, 446.\niv. 2, 5.\niii. 40, 2.\nii. 5.\nCf. Weber, Indian Literature, 70, 147; Zimmer, Altindisches Leben, 129; Whitney, Translation of the Atharvaveda, 259, 260.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-vRRiSha", "mahaa-vRSha", "mahaa-vRiSha", "mahaa-vRuSha", "mahaavRRiSha", "mahaavRSha", "mahaavRiSha", "mahaavRuSha", "mahā", "mahā-vṛṣa", "mahāvṛṣa", "vRRiSha", "vRSha", "vRiSha", "vRuSha", "vṛṣa", "महा", "महावृष", "वृष", "మహా", "మహా-వృష", "మహావృష", "వృష", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ವೃಷ", "ಮಹಾವೃಷ", "ವೃಷ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-vRRiSha", "mahaa-vRSha", "mahaa-vRiSha", "mahaa-vRuSha", "mahaavRRiSha", "mahaavRSha", "mahaavRiSha", "mahaavRuSha", "mahā", "mahā-vṛṣa", "mahāvṛṣa", "vRRiSha", "vRSha", "vRiSha", "vRuSha", "vṛṣa", "mahA", "mahAvfza", "vfza", "మహా", "మహా-వృష", "మహావృష", "వృష", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ವೃಷ", "ಮಹಾವೃಷ", "ವೃಷ"]}], "maha-sala": [{"id": "mahA-SAla", "headword": "महा-शाल", "headword_slp1": "mahA-SAla", "fold": "maha-sala", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "1. Mahā-śāla (lit., ‘having a great house’), a ‘great householder,’ is an expression applied in the Chāndogya upaniṣad (v. 11, 1) to the Brahmins who were instructed by aśvapati, no doubt to emphasize their importance. Cf. mahābrāhmaṇa.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-shaala", "mahaa-shaala_1", "mahaashaala", "mahā", "mahā-śāla", "mahā-śāla_1", "mahāśāla", "shaala", "śāla", "महा", "महा-शाल", "महाशाल", "शाल", "మహా", "మహా-శాల", "మహా-శాల_౧", "మహాశాల", "శాల", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ", "ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ_೧", "ಮಹಾಶಾಲ", "ಶಾಲ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-shaala", "mahaa-shaala_1", "mahaashaala", "mahā", "mahā-śāla", "mahā-śāla_1", "mahāśāla", "shaala", "śāla", "mahA", "mahA-SAla", "mahASAla", "SAla", "మహా", "మహా-శాల", "మహా-శాల_౧", "మహాశాల", "శాల", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ", "ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ_೧", "ಮಹಾಶಾಲ", "ಶಾಲ"]}], "maha-sala jabala": [{"id": "mahA-SAla jAbAla", "headword": "महा-शाल जाबाल", "headword_slp1": "mahA-SAla jAbAla", "fold": "maha-sala jabala", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "2. Mahā-śāla Jābāla is the name of a teacher twice mentioned in the Śatapatha brāhmaṇa, once as instructing dhīra śātaparṇeya, and once as one of the Brahmins who received instruction from aśvapati. In the parallel passage of the Chāndogya upaniṣad the name is prācīnaśāla aupamanyava. The word must be considered a proper name rather than an adjective (1. mahāśāla), as it is taken in the St. Petersburg Dictionary.\nFoot notes x. 3, 3, 1.\nx. 6, 1, 1.\nv. 11, 1.\nCf. Eggeling, Sacred Books of the East, 43, 393, n. 1.\nIn Muṇḍaka upaniṣad, i. 1, 3, the word is used of śaunaka, perhaps merely as an epithet. Cf. Weber, Indian Literature, 161.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-shaala jaabaala", "mahaa-shaala jaabaala_2", "mahaashaala jaabaala", "mahā", "mahā-śāla jābāla", "mahā-śāla jābāla_2", "mahāśāla jābāla", "shaala jaabaala", "śāla jābāla", "महा", "महा-शाल जाबाल", "महाशाल जाबाल", "शाल जाबाल", "మహా", "మహా-శాల జాబాల", "మహా-శాల జాబాల_౨", "మహాశాల జాబాల", "శాల జాబాల", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ", "ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ_೨", "ಮಹಾಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ", "ಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-shaala jaabaala", "mahaa-shaala jaabaala_2", "mahaashaala jaabaala", "mahā", "mahā-śāla jābāla", "mahā-śāla jābāla_2", "mahāśāla jābāla", "shaala jaabaala", "śāla jābāla", "mahA", "mahA-SAla jAbAla", "mahASAla jAbAla", "SAla jAbAla", "మహా", "మహా-శాల జాబాల", "మహా-శాల జాబాల_౨", "మహాశాల జాబాల", "శాల జాబాల", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ", "ಮಹಾ-ಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ_೨", "ಮಹಾಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ", "ಶಾಲ ಜಾಬಾಲ"]}], "maha-suparRa": [{"id": "mahA-suparRa", "headword": "महा-सुपर्ण", "headword_slp1": "mahA-suparRa", "fold": "maha-suparRa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-suparṇa in the Śatapatha brāhmaṇa (xii. 2, 3, 7) denotes a ‘great bird’ or ‘great eagle.’", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-suparNa", "mahaasuparNa", "mahā", "mahā-suparṇa", "mahāsuparṇa", "suparNa", "suparṇa", "महा", "महासुपर्ण", "सुपर्ण", "మహా", "మహా-సుపర్ణ", "మహాసుపర్ణ", "సుపర్ణ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಸುಪರ್ಣ", "ಮಹಾಸುಪರ್ಣ", "ಸುಪರ್ಣ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-suparNa", "mahaasuparNa", "mahā", "mahā-suparṇa", "mahāsuparṇa", "suparNa", "suparṇa", "mahA", "mahAsuparRa", "suparRa", "మహా", "మహా-సుపర్ణ", "మహాసుపర్ణ", "సుపర్ణ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಸುಪರ್ಣ", "ಮಹಾಸುಪರ್ಣ", "ಸುಪರ್ಣ"]}], "maha-suhaya": [{"id": "mahA-suhaya", "headword": "महा-सुहय", "headword_slp1": "mahA-suhaya", "fold": "maha-suhaya", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-suhaya, a ‘great (i.e., high-spirited) horse,’ is the description in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka upaniṣad of the steed from the Indus (saindhava) which tears away the peg of its hobble (paḍbīśa-śaṅkhu).\nFoot notes vi. 2, 13. Cf. śāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, ix. 7; Chāndogya upaniṣad, v. 1, 12; Pischel, Vedische Studien, 1, 234, 235; Keith, śāṅkhāyana Āraṇyaka, 57, n. 3. Cf. Paḍhīśa.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-suhaya", "mahaasuhaya", "mahā", "mahā-suhaya", "mahāsuhaya", "suhaya", "महा", "महासुहय", "सुहय", "మహా", "మహా-సుహయ", "మహాసుహయ", "సుహయ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಸುಹಯ", "ಮಹಾಸುಹಯ", "ಸುಹಯ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-suhaya", "mahaasuhaya", "mahā", "mahā-suhaya", "mahāsuhaya", "suhaya", "mahA", "mahAsuhaya", "suhaya", "మహా", "మహా-సుహయ", "మహాసుహయ", "సుహయ", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಸುಹಯ", "ಮಹಾಸುಹಯ", "ಸುಹಯ"]}], "maha-sukta": [{"id": "mahA-sUkta", "headword": "महा-सूक्त", "headword_slp1": "mahA-sUkta", "fold": "maha-sukta", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahā-sūkta, m. plur., the ‘composers of the long hymns’ of the tenth Maṇḍala of the Rigveda are mentioned in the aitareya Āraṇyaka and the Sūtras. Cf. kṣudra-sūkta.\nFoot notes x. 1-128.\nii. 2, 2.\nAśvalāyana gṛhya sūtra, iii. 4, 2; śāṅkhāyana gṛhya sūtra, iv. 10.\nCf. Weber, Indische Studien, 1, 115; 390; Roth, Zur Litteratur und Geschichte des Weda, 27.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaa", "mahaa-sookta", "mahaa-suukta", "mahaasookta", "mahaasuukta", "mahā", "mahā-sūkta", "mahāsūkta", "sookta", "suukta", "sūkta", "महा", "महासूक्त", "सूक्त", "మహా", "మహా-సూక్త", "మహాసూక్త", "సూక్త", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಸೂಕ್ತ", "ಮಹಾಸೂಕ್ತ", "ಸೂಕ್ತ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaa", "mahaa-sookta", "mahaa-suukta", "mahaasookta", "mahaasuukta", "mahā", "mahā-sūkta", "mahāsūkta", "sookta", "suukta", "sūkta", "mahA", "mahAsUkta", "sUkta", "మహా", "మహా-సూక్త", "మహాసూక్త", "సూక్త", "ಮಹಾ", "ಮಹಾ-ಸೂಕ್ತ", "ಮಹಾಸೂಕ್ತ", "ಸೂಕ್ತ"]}], "mahahna": [{"id": "mahAhna", "headword": "महाह्न", "headword_slp1": "mahAhna", "fold": "mahahna", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahāhna in the kauṣītaki brāhmaṇa (ii. 9) denotes the ‘advanced (time of the) day’--that is, ‘afternoon.’ Cf. mahārātra.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaahna", "mahāhna", "మహాహ్న", "ಮಹಾಹ್ನ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaahna", "mahāhna", "మహాహ్న", "ಮಹಾಹ್ನ"]}], "mahi-dasa Etareya": [{"id": "mahi-dAsa Etareya", "headword": "महि-दास ऐतरेय", "headword_slp1": "mahi-dAsa Etareya", "fold": "mahi-dasa Etareya", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahi-dāsa Aitareya (‘descendant of Itara or Itarā’) is the name of the sage from whom the aitareya brāhmaṇa and Āraṇyaka take their names. He is several times referred to in the aitareya Āraṇyaka, but not as its author. He is credited with a life of 116 years in the Chāndogya upaniṣad and the Jaiminīya upaniṣad brāhmaṇa\nFoot notes ii. 1, 8; 3, 7.\niii. 16, 7.\niv. 2, 11 (cf. Journal of the American Oriental Society, 15, 246).\nCf. Keith, aitareya Āraṇyaka, 16, 17.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["daasa aitareya", "dāsa aitareya", "mahi", "mahi-daasa aitareya", "mahi-dāsa aitareya", "mahidaasa aitareya", "mahidāsa aitareya", "दास ऐतरेय", "महि", "महिदास ऐतरेय", "దాస ఐతరేయ", "మహి", "మహి-దాస ఐతరేయ", "మహిదాస ఐతరేయ", "ದಾಸ ಐತರೇಯ", "ಮಹಿ", "ಮಹಿ-ದಾಸ ಐತರೇಯ", "ಮಹಿದಾಸ ಐತರೇಯ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["daasa aitareya", "dāsa aitareya", "mahi", "mahi-daasa aitareya", "mahi-dāsa aitareya", "mahidaasa aitareya", "mahidāsa aitareya", "dAsa Etareya", "mahi", "mahidAsa Etareya", "దాస ఐతరేయ", "మహి", "మహి-దాస ఐతరేయ", "మహిదాస ఐతరేయ", "ದಾಸ ಐತರೇಯ", "ಮಹಿ", "ಮಹಿ-ದಾಸ ಐತರೇಯ", "ಮಹಿದಾಸ ಐತರೇಯ"]}], "mahisa": [{"id": "mahiza", "headword": "महिष", "headword_slp1": "mahiza", "fold": "mahisa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahiṣa, the ‘strong,’ with or without mṛga, ‘wild beast,’ denotes the ‘buffalo’ in the Rigveda and the later texts. The feminine, mahiṣī, is found in the later Saṃhitās.\nFoot notes Rv. viii. 58, 15; ix. 92, 6; 96, 6; x. 123, 4.\nRv. v. 29, 7; vi. 67, 11; viii. 12, 8; 66, 10; ix. 87, 7; x. 28, 10; 189, 2; Vājasaneyi Saṃhitā, xxiv. 28, etc.\nkāṭhaka Saṃhitā, xxv. 6; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, iii. 8, 5; Ṣaḍviṃśa brāhmaṇa, v. 7, 11.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahiSha", "mahiṣa", "మహిష", "ಮಹಿಷ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahiSha", "mahiṣa", "మహిష", "ಮಹಿಷ"]}], "mahisi": [{"id": "mahizI", "headword": "महिषी", "headword_slp1": "mahizI", "fold": "mahisi", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "1. Mahiṣī. See mahiṣa.", "gloss_language": "en"}, {"gloss": "2. Mahiṣī, ‘the powerful one,’ the name of the first of the four wives (see pati) of the king, is mentioned frequently in the later literature. Perhaps even in the Rigveda the technical sense of ‘first wife’ is present.\nFoot notes taittirīya Saṃhitā, i. 8, 9, 1; kāṭhaka Saṃhitā, xv. 4; Maitrāyaṇī Saṃhitā, ii. 6, 5; pañcaviṃśa brāhmaṇa, xix. 1, 4; Śatapatha brāhmaṇa, vi. 5, 3, 1; vii. 5, 1, 6, etc.\nv. 2, 2; 37, 3.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahiShee", "mahiShee_1", "mahiShee_2", "mahiShi", "mahiShii", "mahiShii_1", "mahiShii_2", "mahiṣi", "mahiṣī", "mahiṣī_1", "mahiṣī_2", "महिषि", "महिषी", "మహిషి", "మహిషీ", "మహిషీ_౧", "మహిషీ_౨", "ಮಹಿಷಿ", "ಮಹಿಷೀ", "ಮಹಿಷೀ_೧", "ಮಹಿಷೀ_೨"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahiShee", "mahiShee_1", "mahiShee_2", "mahiShi", "mahiShii", "mahiShii_1", "mahiShii_2", "mahiṣi", "mahiṣī", "mahiṣī_1", "mahiṣī_2", "mahizi", "mahizI", "మహిషి", "మహిషీ", "మహిషీ_౧", "మహిషీ_౨", "ಮಹಿಷಿ", "ಮಹಿಷೀ", "ಮಹಿಷೀ_೧", "ಮಹಿಷೀ_೨"]}], "mahEtareya": [{"id": "mahEtareya", "headword": "महैतरेय", "headword_slp1": "mahEtareya", "fold": "mahEtareya", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahaitareya is the title of a Vedic text according to the gṛhya Sūtras of the ṛgveda.\nFoot notes Āśvalāyana gṛhya sūtra, iii. 4, 4; of a teacher, in śāṅkhāyana gṛhya sūtra, iv. 10; vi. 1. Cf. Keith, aitareya Āraṇyaka, 39; Oldenberg, Sacred Books of the East, 29, 3, 4.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahaitareya", "మహైతరేయ", "ಮಹೈತರೇಯ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahaitareya", "మహైతరేయ", "ಮಹೈತರೇಯ"]}], "mahoksa": [{"id": "mahokza", "headword": "महोक्ष", "headword_slp1": "mahokza", "fold": "mahoksa", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Mahokṣa, a ‘great bull,’ is mentioned in the Śatapatha brāhmaṇa (iii. 4, 1, 2).", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["mahokSha", "mahokṣa", "mahoxa", "మహోక్ష", "ಮಹೋಕ್ಷ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["mahokSha", "mahokṣa", "mahoxa", "మహోక్ష", "ಮಹೋಕ್ಷ"]}], "mahaki": [{"id": "mAhaki", "headword": "माहकि", "headword_slp1": "mAhaki", "fold": "mahaki", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Māhaki, ‘descendant of Mahaka,’ is the patronymic of a teacher in the vaṃśa brāhmaṇa.\nFoot notes Indische Studien, 4, 382.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["maahaki", "māhaki", "మాహకి", "ಮಾಹಕಿ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["maahaki", "māhaki", "మాహకి", "ಮಾಹಕಿ"]}], "maha-camasya": [{"id": "mAhA-camasya", "headword": "माहा-चमस्य", "headword_slp1": "mAhA-camasya", "fold": "maha-camasya", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Māhā-camasya, ‘descendant of Mahācamasa,’ is the patronymic of a teacher to whom the taittirīya Āraṇyaka ascribes the addition of Mahas to the triad Bhūr Bhuvas svar.\nFoot notes i. 5, 1.\nCf. Keith, aitareya Āraṇyaka, 180.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["camasya", "chamasya", "maahaa", "maahaa-chamasya", "maahaachamasya", "māhā", "māhā-camasya", "māhācamasya", "चमस्य", "माहा", "माहाचमस्य", "చమస్య", "మాహా", "మాహా-చమస్య", "మాహాచమస్య", "ಚಮಸ್ಯ", "ಮಾಹಾ", "ಮಾಹಾ-ಚಮಸ್ಯ", "ಮಾಹಾಚಮಸ್ಯ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["camasya", "chamasya", "maahaa", "maahaa-chamasya", "maahaachamasya", "māhā", "māhā-camasya", "māhācamasya", "camasya", "mAhA", "mAhAcamasya", "చమస్య", "మాహా", "మాహా-చమస్య", "మాహాచమస్య", "ಚಮಸ್ಯ", "ಮಾಹಾ", "ಮಾಹಾ-ಚಮಸ್ಯ", "ಮಾಹಾಚಮಸ್ಯ"]}], "maha-rajana": [{"id": "mAhA-rajana", "headword": "माहा-रजन", "headword_slp1": "mAhA-rajana", "fold": "maha-rajana", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Māhā-rajana, ‘dyed with saffron’ (mahā-rajana), is applied to a garment (vāsas) in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka upaniṣad (ii. 3, 10).", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["maahaa", "maahaa-rajana", "maahaarajana", "māhā", "māhā-rajana", "māhārajana", "rajana", "माहा", "माहारजन", "रजन", "మాహా", "మాహా-రజన", "మాహారజన", "రజన", "ಮಾಹಾ", "ಮಾಹಾ-ರಜನ", "ಮಾಹಾರಜನ", "ರಜನ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["maahaa", "maahaa-rajana", "maahaarajana", "māhā", "māhā-rajana", "māhārajana", "rajana", "mAhA", "mAhArajana", "rajana", "మాహా", "మాహా-రజన", "మాహారజన", "రజన", "ಮಾಹಾ", "ಮಾಹಾ-ರಜನ", "ಮಾಹಾರಜನ", "ರಜನ"]}], "maha-rajya": [{"id": "mAhA-rAjya", "headword": "माहा-राज्य", "headword_slp1": "mAhA-rAjya", "fold": "maha-rajya", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Māhā-rājya, ‘the dignity of a great king’ (mahā-rāja), is mentioned in the aitareya brāhmaṇa (viii. 6, 5; 12, 4; 15, 3).", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["maahaa", "maahaa-raajya", "maahaaraajya", "māhā", "māhā-rājya", "māhārājya", "raajya", "rājya", "माहा", "माहाराज्य", "राज्य", "మాహా", "మాహా-రాజ్య", "మాహారాజ్య", "రాజ్య", "ಮಾಹಾ", "ಮಾಹಾ-ರಾಜ್ಯ", "ಮಾಹಾರಾಜ್ಯ", "ರಾಜ್ಯ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["maahaa", "maahaa-raajya", "maahaaraajya", "māhā", "māhā-rājya", "māhārājya", "raajya", "rājya", "mAhA", "mAhArAjya", "rAjya", "మాహా", "మాహా-రాజ్య", "మాహారాజ్య", "రాజ్య", "ಮಾಹಾ", "ಮಾಹಾ-ರಾಜ್ಯ", "ಮಾಹಾರಾಜ್ಯ", "ರಾಜ್ಯ"]}], "mahitTi": [{"id": "mAhitTi", "headword": "माहित्थि", "headword_slp1": "mAhitTi", "fold": "mahitTi", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Māhitthi, ‘descendant of Mahittha,’ is the patronymic of a teacher mentioned several times in the Śatapatha brāhmaṇa. He is said to be a pupil of vāmakakṣāyaṇa in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka upaniṣad.\nFoot notes vi. 2, 2, 10; viii. 6, 1, 16 et seq.; ix. 5, 1, 57; x. 6, 5, 9.\nvi. 5, 4 kāṇva.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["maahitthi", "māhitthi", "మాహిత్థి", "ಮಾಹಿತ್ಥಿ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["maahitthi", "māhitthi", "మాహిత్థి", "ಮಾಹಿತ್ಥಿ"]}], "mahina": [{"id": "mAhIna", "headword": "माहीन", "headword_slp1": "mAhIna", "fold": "mahina", "headword_language": "sa", "source": "vedic-index-macdonell-keith", "senses": [{"gloss": "Māhīna occurs in one passage of the Rigveda, which celebrates Asamāti as a king. The word, used in the plural, may be a patronymic referring to the priests who praised Asamāti, or it may be an adjective of uncertain meaning.\nFoot notes x. 60, 1. Cf. Ludwig, Translation of the Rigveda, 3, 138.", "gloss_language": "en"}], "synonyms": ["maaheena", "maahiina", "māhīna", "మాహీన", "ಮಾಹೀನ"], "synonyms_slp1": ["maaheena", "maahiina", "māhīna", "మాహీన", "ಮಾಹೀನ"]}]}